WEBVTT

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.802
He hasn't left me
with a single shot.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.671
In that case, pal,
call a safety.

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:09.675
Safety?

00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:11.176
Yeah, it's a defensive strategy.

00:00:11.211 --> 00:00:12.576
Since you can't make a shot,

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:14.239
the idea is to leave
the cue ball

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.406
somewhere on the table

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:17.643
where Tuvok can't make a shot,
either.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.845
I don't know.
It sounds cowardly.

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:21.979
Suit yourself, pal,
but, uh, if you leave

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:23.988
"Vulcan Slim" over here
with an open shot,

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.322
he's got a very good chance
of running the table.

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:30.922
All right... safety.

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:36.702
Ah!

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:42.468
Mr. Vulcan, let's see you reason
your way out of this conundrum.

00:00:42.509 --> 00:00:43.476
This safety business

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.637
is a lot more satisfying
than I imagined.

00:00:45.678 --> 00:00:48.374
I've left him
with an impossible shot.

00:00:48.415 --> 00:00:50.576
The shot may be difficult,
Mr. Neelix,

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.211
but to say that it is impossible
is an exaggeration.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.652
Go ahead, then.

00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:56.154
Call your shot.

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:59.750
Logic would dictate that if
I strike the cue ball properly,

00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:03.285
it will deflect first off
the rear bumper, then the side,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:05.160
striking the 11 ball
and causing it to roll

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:06.597
directly into the corner pocket.

00:01:06.633 --> 00:01:09.193
This I have to see.

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.310
Perhaps the ship's stabilizers

00:01:19.345 --> 00:01:21.074
are not operating
at peak efficiency.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:23.981
Yeah, and maybe Tom Terrific
over here forgot to tell you

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.917
that Sandrine's table rolls
a little to the "east."

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.353
You should have called a safety.

00:01:29.389 --> 00:01:33.291
Janeway to Mr. Neelix.
Please report to the Bridge.

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:42.961
You wanted to see me, Captain?

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:43.934
Yes, Neelix.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.699
We've received
a subspace message

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:47.171
from an approaching vessel.

00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:49.266
They're asking for you.

00:01:49.309 --> 00:01:50.276
Me?

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.744
The alien ship is entering
visual range, Captain.

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.579
Slow to impulse.
On screen.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:00.580
Do you recognize it?

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.953
That's a Haakonian shuttle.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:04.957
Haakonian?

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:10.896
My people were at war with them
for the better part of a decade.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.160
They...

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:16.965
conquered my homeworld
more than 15 years ago.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:20.665
Any idea what the Haakonians
want with you now?

00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:22.698
None at all.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.437
The shuttle is hailing us,
Captain.

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:25.671
Open a channel.

00:02:25.712 --> 00:02:26.770
Channel open.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:28.508
I'm Captain Kathryn Janeway

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:30.914
of the Federation
Starship Voyager.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:31.974
What can we do for you?

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:34.349
As I stated in my message,
Captain,

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:37.550
I understand you have
a Talaxian called Neelix

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.785
aboard your vessel.

00:02:39.826 --> 00:02:41.953
I'm Neelix.

00:02:41.995 --> 00:02:43.587
What do you want?

00:02:43.630 --> 00:02:46.997
It is a matter
of utmost urgency.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:51.366
However, I would prefer
to speak with you privately.

00:02:51.404 --> 00:02:53.372
Your life may very well
depend upon it.

00:02:56.609 --> 00:02:58.702
Who are you?

00:02:58.745 --> 00:03:00.770
Forgive me.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:02.781
I am Jetrel.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:04.942
Dr. Ma'Bor Jetrel.

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.313
Neelix...

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:06.033
He's a mass murderer!

00:05:08.572 --> 00:05:11.234
When I was much younger,

00:05:11.275 --> 00:05:15.041
my family and I lived
on a moon called Rinax...

00:05:15.079 --> 00:05:18.412
a colony with
the most temperate climate

00:05:18.449 --> 00:05:20.644
in the entire Talaxian system.

00:05:20.684 --> 00:05:22.811
Warm days, balmy nights...

00:05:22.853 --> 00:05:25.947
until the metreon cascade.

00:05:26.023 --> 00:05:28.491
Melodic name, isn't it?

00:05:28.526 --> 00:05:32.189
Especially for a weapon
of mass destruction.

00:05:32.229 --> 00:05:34.857
And Jetrel was somehow
involved with this weapon?

00:05:37.835 --> 00:05:41.396
Dr. Jetrel was the scientist
who conceived

00:05:41.438 --> 00:05:44.532
the metreon cascade,
then he led the team

00:05:44.575 --> 00:05:47.237
of scientists who built it.

00:05:47.278 --> 00:05:48.540
I see.

00:05:48.579 --> 00:05:50.342
In the blink of an eye,

00:05:50.381 --> 00:05:53.077
Rinax was enveloped
by a deadly cloud

00:05:53.117 --> 00:05:54.982
and those lovely days

00:05:55.085 --> 00:05:58.316
were turned into one endless,
frigid night.

00:06:00.391 --> 00:06:04.395
More than 300,000 were killed.

00:06:04.528 --> 00:06:07.793
But you... survived.

00:06:07.831 --> 00:06:13.360
I had the good fortune
to be on Talax at the time,

00:06:13.404 --> 00:06:15.338
with our defense forces,

00:06:15.372 --> 00:06:18.864
preparing for an invasion
that never came.

00:06:18.909 --> 00:06:21.139
It wasn't necessary.

00:06:21.178 --> 00:06:24.909
The day after the cascade
was deployed,

00:06:24.948 --> 00:06:26.347
Talax surrendered
unconditionally

00:06:26.383 --> 00:06:28.647
to the Haakonian Order.

00:06:31.922 --> 00:06:33.890
And your family?

00:06:40.631 --> 00:06:43.031
I am so sorry.

00:06:46.103 --> 00:06:47.968
Dr. Jetrel.

00:06:48.071 --> 00:06:50.539
Lieutenant Tuvok,
my Chief of Security.

00:06:50.574 --> 00:06:53.805
I have heard of your transporter
technology, Captain,

00:06:53.844 --> 00:06:59.146
but to experience it firsthand
is truly remarkable.

00:06:59.183 --> 00:07:01.481
Mr. Neelix has declined
to meet with you.

00:07:01.518 --> 00:07:02.917
That's not surprising.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.682
I'm afraid I am not
a very popular figure

00:07:05.722 --> 00:07:07.417
among the Talaxian people.

00:07:07.458 --> 00:07:10.393
May we show you
to your quarters?

00:07:13.397 --> 00:07:15.194
Mr. Neelix has
given me permission

00:07:15.232 --> 00:07:16.699
to speak on his behalf.

00:07:16.733 --> 00:07:19.930
You indicated that he may be
in some kind of danger...

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:21.938
Yes. It's imperative
that he undergo

00:07:21.972 --> 00:07:23.496
a complete medical examination.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:24.802
For what purpose?

00:07:24.842 --> 00:07:27.333
Our war records show
that he was part of the team

00:07:27.377 --> 00:07:29.402
that returned to Rinax
after the cascade

00:07:29.446 --> 00:07:31.073
in order to evacuate survivors.

00:07:31.114 --> 00:07:33.912
He was exposed
to high concentrations

00:07:33.951 --> 00:07:35.441
of metreon isotopes.

00:07:35.486 --> 00:07:39.047
In recent years, several
of the rescuers have developed

00:07:39.089 --> 00:07:42.422
a degenerative blood disease
called metremia.

00:07:42.459 --> 00:07:43.858
Is it serious?

00:07:43.894 --> 00:07:47.091
I am afraid that it's fatal.

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:50.429
The disease attacks its victims
on a molecular level.

00:07:50.467 --> 00:07:51.866
It may lay dormant for years,

00:07:51.902 --> 00:07:54.063
but once it manifests itself,

00:07:54.104 --> 00:07:57.335
it will cause
the body's atomic structure

00:07:57.374 --> 00:07:58.864
to undergo fission.

00:07:58.909 --> 00:08:01.070
The cells will begin
to disintegrate.

00:08:01.111 --> 00:08:03.272
My equipment
is specifically designed

00:08:03.313 --> 00:08:06.749
to detect the subatomic
signature of the disorder.

00:08:06.783 --> 00:08:09.183
May I ask, Doctor,
why you've taken it

00:08:09.219 --> 00:08:11.449
upon yourself to examine
Mr. Neelix?

00:08:11.488 --> 00:08:14.286
I've evaluated
as many members of the team

00:08:14.324 --> 00:08:17.521
as possible, collecting data,
in hopes that one day,

00:08:17.561 --> 00:08:20.359
my research will lead
to a cure for metremia.

00:08:20.397 --> 00:08:23.366
Please, try to persuade
Mr. Neelix to see me.

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:25.630
Each Talaxian I screen

00:08:25.669 --> 00:08:29.571
brings me one step closer
to a cure.

00:08:42.119 --> 00:08:45.646
Neelix, why didn't you ever
tell me about the war?

00:08:45.689 --> 00:08:51.889
Maybe this is one experience
that can't be shared.

00:08:51.929 --> 00:08:54.420
It's too...

00:08:54.464 --> 00:08:56.432
hard to...

00:08:56.466 --> 00:08:57.933
describe how I feel

00:08:57.968 --> 00:09:01.131
to someone who didn't
see what I saw.

00:09:01.171 --> 00:09:03.901
I'm sorry.

00:09:03.974 --> 00:09:07.137
You have nothing to be
sorry about, Neelix.

00:09:09.079 --> 00:09:10.603
Excuse me.

00:09:10.647 --> 00:09:14.640
Captain, back from your doctor's
appointment already?

00:09:14.685 --> 00:09:19.418
I'm afraid I have some...
disturbing news, Neelix.

00:09:19.456 --> 00:09:21.856
Nothing you tell me
could make this day

00:09:21.892 --> 00:09:24.452
any more disturbing
than it's already been.

00:09:29.533 --> 00:09:33.936
According to Jetrel,
you were exposed

00:09:33.971 --> 00:09:36.496
to dangerous levels
of metreon poisoning

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.703
and may be at risk of developing
a fatal blood disorder.

00:09:39.743 --> 00:09:43.042
He wants to screen you
for the disease.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:51.487
Uh, Captain...

00:09:51.521 --> 00:09:56.151
please tell Dr. Jetrel
that I am... touched

00:09:56.193 --> 00:09:58.661
by his tender concern
for my state of health,

00:09:58.695 --> 00:10:00.526
but that I'd rather be immersed

00:10:00.564 --> 00:10:04.933
in a pit of Krallinian eels
than be examined by him.

00:10:04.968 --> 00:10:07.402
Neelix, if there's
something wrong with you,

00:10:07.437 --> 00:10:08.904
we should find out.

00:10:08.939 --> 00:10:11.965
I don't want that man
within ten parsecs of me!

00:10:12.042 --> 00:10:13.509
I understand your feelings,

00:10:13.543 --> 00:10:15.738
but this is your life
we're talking about.

00:10:15.779 --> 00:10:20.375
Don't either of you find it
the slightest bit strange

00:10:20.417 --> 00:10:23.545
that a man who has made
it his life's work

00:10:23.587 --> 00:10:24.849
to develop a weapon

00:10:24.888 --> 00:10:27.584
to destroy as many
Talaxians as possible

00:10:27.624 --> 00:10:31.560
should suddenly be concerned
with this Talaxian's health?

00:10:31.595 --> 00:10:34.120
I don't know
what his motives are.

00:10:34.164 --> 00:10:37.964
Maybe he's trying
to undo some of the damage

00:10:38.001 --> 00:10:41.198
his weapon caused,
but he seems sincere, Neelix,

00:10:41.238 --> 00:10:43.604
and at this point,
I have no reason to doubt him.

00:10:46.109 --> 00:10:48.202
If the disease is fatal,

00:10:48.245 --> 00:10:51.373
what's the point of knowing
whether I have it or not?

00:10:51.415 --> 00:10:53.849
Our Doctor is
the most skilled physician

00:10:53.884 --> 00:10:55.909
either of us has ever met.

00:10:55.952 --> 00:10:57.647
If you have this disease,

00:10:57.688 --> 00:11:01.385
he won't stop until he's found
some way to treat it.

00:11:01.425 --> 00:11:04.155
And if Jetrel discovers
you don't have the disease,

00:11:04.194 --> 00:11:07.686
at least you'll have
peace of mind.

00:11:07.731 --> 00:11:10.564
Peace of mind
is a relative thing, Captain.

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:14.696
I simply want you to hear
what he has to say,

00:11:14.738 --> 00:11:17.571
then if you still don't
want him to examine you,

00:11:17.607 --> 00:11:19.802
no one will force you.

00:11:19.843 --> 00:11:23.973
Oh, outnumbered and outflanked.

00:11:24.081 --> 00:11:26.481
All right, then.

00:11:26.516 --> 00:11:28.245
I surrender.

00:11:35.258 --> 00:11:37.317
The metreon isotopes are unique.

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:39.658
Their rate of decay
is highly variable.

00:11:39.696 --> 00:11:41.994
We have found
that it's sometimes years

00:11:42.065 --> 00:11:45.000
before metremia sets in,
and unfortunately...

00:11:45.035 --> 00:11:47.503
Why are you doing this?

00:11:47.537 --> 00:11:49.232
I beg your pardon?

00:11:49.272 --> 00:11:53.436
Is it all just
scientific curiosity?

00:11:53.477 --> 00:11:56.275
Metreons and isotopes?

00:11:56.313 --> 00:12:02.809
Or do you feel... guilty
about what you did?

00:12:02.853 --> 00:12:06.380
Guilty.

00:12:06.423 --> 00:12:08.391
I do not regret it.

00:12:08.425 --> 00:12:10.655
I did what had to be done.

00:12:10.694 --> 00:12:13.288
Really? It was necessary
to vaporize

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:15.093
more than a quarter
of a million people

00:12:15.132 --> 00:12:16.292
and to leave thousands of others

00:12:16.333 --> 00:12:18.401
to be eaten away
by metreon poisoning?

00:12:18.534 --> 00:12:21.162
Would it make any difference
if I told you

00:12:21.203 --> 00:12:24.434
we never thought there would be
any radiation poisoning...

00:12:24.473 --> 00:12:27.704
that anyone close enough
to be exposed

00:12:27.743 --> 00:12:29.973
would be killed
by the initial blast?

00:12:30.012 --> 00:12:32.105
It was unfortunate
we were wrong.

00:12:32.148 --> 00:12:33.342
Unfortunate?!

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.145
Did you hear that, Captain?

00:12:37.186 --> 00:12:38.847
It was "unfortunate."

00:12:38.888 --> 00:12:42.380
Neelix, I think
what Dr. Jetrel is trying...

00:12:42.425 --> 00:12:43.517
No, Captain.
That's all right.

00:12:43.559 --> 00:12:45.390
I'm used to it.

00:12:45.428 --> 00:12:47.259
I'm simply a scientist.

00:12:47.296 --> 00:12:50.891
Yes, I developed the weapon,

00:12:50.933 --> 00:12:55.495
but it was the government
and the military leaders

00:12:55.538 --> 00:12:58.735
who decided to use it, not I.

00:12:58.774 --> 00:13:03.677
That must be a very convenient
distinction for you.

00:13:03.712 --> 00:13:06.237
Does it help you sleep at night?

00:13:06.282 --> 00:13:08.750
I slept no worse last night

00:13:08.784 --> 00:13:13.312
than I have any other night
for the past 15 years.

00:13:13.355 --> 00:13:15.482
What is that supposed to mean?

00:13:15.524 --> 00:13:16.889
It means I must

00:13:16.926 --> 00:13:21.386
live with my conscience
as you must live with yours.

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:25.457
How many did you kill
during the war?

00:13:25.501 --> 00:13:26.763
Gentlemen, please.

00:13:28.671 --> 00:13:31.231
This is obviously very difficult
for both of you,

00:13:31.273 --> 00:13:34.174
but we are not here
to debate history.

00:13:34.210 --> 00:13:37.373
We are here to talk
about Neelix's condition.

00:13:37.413 --> 00:13:38.778
Don't worry about it,

00:13:38.814 --> 00:13:42.113
Captain, because Dr. Jetrel
will have to find himself

00:13:42.151 --> 00:13:46.019
another laboratory rodent
to help his experiments.

00:13:48.057 --> 00:13:52.221
Because I would rather die
than help you

00:13:52.261 --> 00:13:54.388
ease your conscience.

00:13:54.430 --> 00:13:57.957
I do not expect you
to like me, Mr. Neelix,

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:01.492
nor do I hope to allay
your obvious pain

00:14:01.537 --> 00:14:03.266
with moral arguments,

00:14:03.305 --> 00:14:05.569
but I do believe
I can help you...

00:14:05.608 --> 00:14:08.304
if not you, others of your race.

00:14:08.344 --> 00:14:12.940
Isn't that more important
than punishing me?

00:14:23.459 --> 00:14:26.917
Are you... sure
you can't do this?

00:14:26.962 --> 00:14:30.796
Your newfound confidence in me
is flattering, Mr. Neelix,

00:14:30.833 --> 00:14:33.734
but Dr. Jetrel has instruments
designed specifically

00:14:33.769 --> 00:14:35.498
to analyze Talaxian physiology.

00:14:37.206 --> 00:14:40.198
Just get it over with.

00:14:45.548 --> 00:14:48.381
Did I ever... tell you

00:14:48.417 --> 00:14:51.181
about the notorious
two-tailed talchoks of Rinax?

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:52.847
I don't think so.

00:14:52.888 --> 00:14:54.788
Nasty little vermin.

00:14:54.823 --> 00:14:56.256
Sharp claws, dripping fangs.

00:14:56.292 --> 00:14:59.557
Ew! It was quite a nuisance.

00:14:59.595 --> 00:15:02.291
So one summer when I was a boy,

00:15:02.331 --> 00:15:05.789
I decided to do
something about them.

00:15:05.834 --> 00:15:07.802
I spent weeks tinkering,

00:15:07.836 --> 00:15:10.930
creating
a foolproof talchok trap.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:14.703
Once I perfected it,
I set it in the garden.

00:15:14.743 --> 00:15:17.940
And the next day,
I found one of those beasts

00:15:18.013 --> 00:15:19.412
pinned at the neck.

00:15:19.448 --> 00:15:21.882
But it wasn't dead.

00:15:21.917 --> 00:15:26.786
It was squirming and squealing
in agony.

00:15:26.822 --> 00:15:29.814
And suddenly, it didn't
look so fiendish anymore.

00:15:29.858 --> 00:15:32.622
It looked like
a poor, innocent animal.

00:15:32.661 --> 00:15:35.630
What a horrible story.

00:15:35.664 --> 00:15:38.428
Well, you see, I had become
so fascinated with my invention

00:15:38.467 --> 00:15:39.764
that I never really thought

00:15:39.802 --> 00:15:41.770
about how that poor creature
would suffer.

00:15:41.804 --> 00:15:43.601
Are you finished?

00:15:43.639 --> 00:15:44.970
For now.

00:15:46.442 --> 00:15:48.137
So am I.

00:15:48.177 --> 00:15:49.508
And?

00:15:49.545 --> 00:15:51.706
I'm sorry, Mr. Neelix.

00:15:51.747 --> 00:15:53.339
Sorry? Why are you sorry?

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:57.244
I'm afraid you have
incipient metremia.

00:16:20.409 --> 00:16:21.535
Come in!

00:16:23.679 --> 00:16:25.306
How are you feeling?

00:16:25.347 --> 00:16:27.406
Kes, my sweet.

00:16:28.751 --> 00:16:31.720
I'll have you know
this is not the first time

00:16:31.754 --> 00:16:34.621
I have stared into
the gaping maw of death.

00:16:34.657 --> 00:16:37.922
Why once, during the Battle
of the Pyrithian Gorge,

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:42.556
I faced down an entire battery
of Haakonian artillery.

00:16:42.598 --> 00:16:46.125
There I was with only
a small crevice for cover...

00:16:46.168 --> 00:16:48.534
Neelix, you're
protecting me again.

00:16:48.570 --> 00:16:52.529
I can understand your not
wanting to talk about Rinax.

00:16:52.574 --> 00:16:54.599
I wasn't there.
You're right.

00:16:54.643 --> 00:16:57.407
But I'm here now with you.

00:16:57.446 --> 00:17:00.176
We have to face this together.

00:17:02.117 --> 00:17:05.814
Well, there's one good thing
that will come from all this.

00:17:05.854 --> 00:17:10.450
When I first met you,
I didn't know that your species

00:17:10.492 --> 00:17:13.017
only lived eight or nine years.

00:17:13.062 --> 00:17:15.622
I fell in love with you

00:17:15.664 --> 00:17:18.724
without knowing
how lonely it would be

00:17:18.767 --> 00:17:21.463
to live without you
after you're gone.

00:17:21.503 --> 00:17:23.937
Now that I'm going
to die first,

00:17:24.006 --> 00:17:26.474
I don't have to worry about it.

00:17:26.508 --> 00:17:27.941
Before I met you,

00:17:28.043 --> 00:17:31.035
eight or nine years
seemed like an eternity.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:34.641
It never occurred to me
that anyone could live longer.

00:17:34.683 --> 00:17:36.241
Now that we're together,

00:17:36.285 --> 00:17:37.946
no matter how many years
we have left,

00:17:38.053 --> 00:17:39.782
it doesn't seem like enough.

00:17:43.092 --> 00:17:45.060
But the important thing is

00:17:45.094 --> 00:17:48.120
to cherish whatever
time we have together.

00:17:49.798 --> 00:17:52.767
Whether it's a day or a decade.

00:18:03.212 --> 00:18:04.645
Come in.

00:18:06.248 --> 00:18:07.943
Am I interrupting?

00:18:10.085 --> 00:18:12.019
Please, sit down.

00:18:13.522 --> 00:18:18.255
Captain, I must tell you,
your ship is simply astounding.

00:18:18.293 --> 00:18:21.091
I have been studying
your transporter system.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:22.791
Transporter systems?

00:18:22.831 --> 00:18:25.425
I would think you'd be
more concerned with Mr. Neelix.

00:18:25.467 --> 00:18:26.798
Forgive me.

00:18:26.835 --> 00:18:29.770
But that is what I am here
to talk about.

00:18:29.805 --> 00:18:33.434
I believe with a relatively
few minor modifications,

00:18:33.475 --> 00:18:35.807
the transporter system
could be used

00:18:35.844 --> 00:18:38.404
to retrieve a sample
of the metreon cloud

00:18:38.447 --> 00:18:39.937
surrounding Rinax.

00:18:39.982 --> 00:18:41.210
To what end?

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:44.242
I've always believed
that if I could isolate

00:18:44.286 --> 00:18:46.686
the free isotope
that causes metremia,

00:18:46.722 --> 00:18:50.180
it could be used
to synthesize an antibody.

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:53.452
Then theoretically,

00:18:53.495 --> 00:18:57.727
a victim's own immune system
could destroy the disease.

00:18:57.766 --> 00:19:00.326
Yes, Captain,
and your transporter system

00:19:00.369 --> 00:19:01.893
makes it all possible.

00:19:01.937 --> 00:19:04.235
Is the isotope stable enough
to be transported?

00:19:04.273 --> 00:19:06.241
I believe so.

00:19:06.275 --> 00:19:08.766
We could construct
a containment field

00:19:08.811 --> 00:19:10.676
to prevent any contamination
of the ship.

00:19:10.712 --> 00:19:12.304
And I could have
our Doctor assist you

00:19:12.347 --> 00:19:13.871
with synthesizing the antibody.

00:19:13.916 --> 00:19:15.543
My thoughts exactly.

00:19:15.584 --> 00:19:17.313
Janeway to Bridge.

00:19:17.352 --> 00:19:18.649
Chakotay here, Captain.

00:19:18.687 --> 00:19:21.679
Have Dr. Jetrel's vessel
tractored into the shuttlebay.

00:19:21.723 --> 00:19:24.248
And lay in a course
for the Talaxian system.

00:19:24.293 --> 00:19:26.523
That's a significant detour,
Captain.

00:19:26.562 --> 00:19:27.996
I'm aware of that, Commander,

00:19:28.129 --> 00:19:30.927
but it may mean saving
Neelix's life.

00:19:30.965 --> 00:19:32.660
Understood.

00:19:32.700 --> 00:19:34.930
I'll send a message to Talax
requesting permission

00:19:35.003 --> 00:19:36.732
to transport material
from the cloud.

00:19:36.771 --> 00:19:39.137
In the meantime, Lieutenant
Torres in Engineering

00:19:39.173 --> 00:19:40.640
can give you a hand

00:19:40.675 --> 00:19:43.337
with the transporter
modifications.

00:19:43.378 --> 00:19:44.936
Thank you, Captain.

00:19:45.046 --> 00:19:47.276
Thank you, Dr. Jetrel.

00:19:47.315 --> 00:19:50.045
It's a very promising idea.

00:19:53.988 --> 00:19:55.478
What is it?

00:19:56.691 --> 00:19:58.682
Nothing.
I just think, uh...

00:19:58.726 --> 00:20:01.661
I'm a little overexcited
about the prospects

00:20:01.696 --> 00:20:05.188
of discovering a cure,
and all that sparring

00:20:05.233 --> 00:20:07.428
with Mr. Neelix
does take a toll.

00:20:07.468 --> 00:20:09.527
Maybe you should let the Doctor
have a look at you.

00:20:09.570 --> 00:20:12.596
No, I'm fine,
and there's so much to be done.

00:20:12.640 --> 00:20:14.301
I want to start right away.

00:20:21.215 --> 00:20:25.276
Captain's Log, Stardate 48832.1.

00:20:25.319 --> 00:20:28.152
Kes has prevailed upon Neelix
to allow Dr. Jetrel

00:20:28.189 --> 00:20:30.123
to continue metabolic scans

00:20:30.158 --> 00:20:32.217
in the hope that it will
facilitate treatment

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:35.855
once the antibody
has been synthesized.

00:20:35.897 --> 00:20:38.923
Will you be needing
my assistance, Doctor?

00:20:38.966 --> 00:20:41.400
Not until we have the isotope.

00:20:41.436 --> 00:20:44.997
Mr. Neelix, is there
anything more I can do for you?

00:20:45.039 --> 00:20:46.939
Very well then.

00:20:46.974 --> 00:20:51.001
Computer, override Command
1-EMH-Alpha and end program.

00:20:52.513 --> 00:20:53.810
Incredible.

00:20:55.783 --> 00:20:58.650
A hologram
that can deactivate itself.

00:20:58.686 --> 00:21:00.711
Is there anything
besides science

00:21:00.755 --> 00:21:03.280
that makes your heart
beat faster, Jetrel?

00:21:03.324 --> 00:21:04.757
Not anymore.

00:21:04.792 --> 00:21:07.386
Now would you please sit down?

00:21:11.499 --> 00:21:15.265
You know
what I've been thinking?

00:21:15.303 --> 00:21:19.399
If I'd been in charge
of the cascade,

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:21.465
I'd have...

00:21:21.509 --> 00:21:23.204
I don't know...

00:21:23.244 --> 00:21:25.269
chosen a military target

00:21:25.313 --> 00:21:30.979
or simply deployed it
on an uninhabited planet.

00:21:31.085 --> 00:21:32.643
Somehow I don't think

00:21:32.687 --> 00:21:35.417
I'd have targeted
innocent civilians.

00:21:35.456 --> 00:21:36.787
The military strategists

00:21:36.824 --> 00:21:39.190
did not think a demonstration
would work.

00:21:39.227 --> 00:21:41.957
They wanted to show
the power of the cascade,

00:21:41.996 --> 00:21:43.395
in all its horror.

00:21:43.431 --> 00:21:47.197
You should have tried
to stop them!

00:21:47.235 --> 00:21:49.328
Why didn't you speak out?

00:21:49.370 --> 00:21:51.668
People would have listened
to you.

00:21:51.706 --> 00:21:56.006
It would not have made
any difference.

00:21:56.043 --> 00:21:58.807
If I had not discovered
the cascade,

00:21:58.846 --> 00:22:00.006
it would have been someone else.

00:22:00.047 --> 00:22:01.571
Don't you see?

00:22:01.616 --> 00:22:03.584
It was a scientific
inevitability...

00:22:03.618 --> 00:22:07.145
one discovery flowing
naturally to the next.

00:22:07.188 --> 00:22:08.951
Something so enormous as science

00:22:09.023 --> 00:22:12.083
will not stop for something
as small as man, Mr. Neelix.

00:22:12.126 --> 00:22:14.959
So you did it for science.

00:22:14.996 --> 00:22:17.089
For my planet.

00:22:17.131 --> 00:22:20.259
And, yes, for science...

00:22:20.301 --> 00:22:23.395
to know whether or not
it could be done.

00:22:23.437 --> 00:22:27.271
It's good to know
how the world works.

00:22:27.308 --> 00:22:29.776
It is not possible
to be a scientist

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:32.404
unless you believe
that all the knowledge

00:22:32.446 --> 00:22:35.074
of the universe and all
the power that it bestows

00:22:35.116 --> 00:22:39.450
is of intrinsic value
to everyone,

00:22:39.487 --> 00:22:43.389
and one must share
that knowledge...

00:22:43.424 --> 00:22:46.325
and allow it to be applied,

00:22:46.360 --> 00:22:51.093
and then be willing
to live with the consequences.

00:22:52.466 --> 00:22:54.366
Consequences?

00:22:54.402 --> 00:22:57.394
Who are you
to talk about consequences?

00:22:57.438 --> 00:23:00.407
You are not the only one
to lose family

00:23:00.441 --> 00:23:02.409
during the war, Mr. Neelix.

00:23:02.443 --> 00:23:04.070
Your family was killed?

00:23:04.111 --> 00:23:06.636
Nothing as clean as death.

00:23:08.816 --> 00:23:12.877
When I returned home,
after the cascade, to my wife...

00:23:12.920 --> 00:23:15.252
my lovely wife Ka'Ree...

00:23:15.289 --> 00:23:19.123
she could not bear
to look at me.

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:23.290
When I would reach out for her,
she would pull away

00:23:23.331 --> 00:23:25.822
as if I had
some contagious disease.

00:23:25.866 --> 00:23:30.803
You see, like you, she thought
I had become a monster.

00:23:30.838 --> 00:23:35.070
Shortly after that,
she took my three children...

00:23:36.277 --> 00:23:39.246
and I have not seen them since.

00:23:39.280 --> 00:23:42.044
That's a sad story, Jetrel.

00:23:43.751 --> 00:23:47.619
But let me tell you another one.

00:23:47.655 --> 00:23:53.321
A man goes back to Rinax
after the cascade,

00:23:53.361 --> 00:23:59.095
back to what had been his home,
to look for survivors.

00:23:59.133 --> 00:24:03.467
But the impact of the blast
has set off hundreds of fires

00:24:03.504 --> 00:24:08.703
and there's nothing there,
just smoldering ruins

00:24:08.743 --> 00:24:11.211
and the stench of seared flesh.

00:24:11.245 --> 00:24:12.872
But in the distance,

00:24:12.913 --> 00:24:15.381
in the middle
of all that emptiness,

00:24:15.416 --> 00:24:17.941
from out of this...

00:24:18.052 --> 00:24:20.919
huge cloud of billowing dust...

00:24:23.190 --> 00:24:27.957
he can see bodies moving,

00:24:28.062 --> 00:24:29.586
whimpering,

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:32.326
coming toward him.

00:24:34.635 --> 00:24:37.433
They're monsters...

00:24:37.471 --> 00:24:40.406
their flesh horribly charred.

00:24:40.441 --> 00:24:42.841
The color of shale.

00:24:46.580 --> 00:24:49.048
One of them comes toward him.

00:24:51.285 --> 00:24:53.617
Mangled arms outstretched.

00:24:55.623 --> 00:24:57.352
And he can't help it.

00:24:57.391 --> 00:25:00.656
He, h-he turns away, frightened.

00:25:00.695 --> 00:25:04.631
But then the thing speaks.

00:25:05.866 --> 00:25:08.926
And he knows by the sound
of her voice

00:25:09.036 --> 00:25:12.938
that she's not a monster
at all, but a child...

00:25:13.007 --> 00:25:15.100
a little girl.

00:25:15.142 --> 00:25:18.236
Mr. Neelix, I...

00:25:22.249 --> 00:25:25.741
Her name was Palaxia.

00:25:27.988 --> 00:25:31.389
We brought her back to Talax
with the other survivors.

00:25:31.425 --> 00:25:36.761
Over the next few weeks,
I stayed at her bedside

00:25:36.797 --> 00:25:38.958
and watched her wither away.

00:25:42.436 --> 00:25:46.463
Those are consequences,
Dr. Jetrel.

00:25:50.578 --> 00:25:52.842
There is no way

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:56.441
I can ever apologize to you,
Mr. Neelix.

00:25:57.952 --> 00:26:00.682
That's why I have not tried.

00:26:03.224 --> 00:26:07.251
Did you ever think
that maybe your wife was right?

00:26:07.294 --> 00:26:09.888
That you had become a monster?

00:26:10.998 --> 00:26:12.124
Yes.

00:26:14.602 --> 00:26:16.934
The day we tested the cascade,

00:26:17.037 --> 00:26:24.170
when I saw that blinding light
brighter than a thousand suns...

00:26:24.211 --> 00:26:29.513
I knew at that moment
exactly what I had become.

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:33.587
I hope you have to live
with that a very long time.

00:26:33.720 --> 00:26:38.487
I'm afraid you will not
get your wish, Mr. Neelix.

00:26:38.525 --> 00:26:40.356
And why is that?

00:26:42.262 --> 00:26:44.059
I have advanced metremia.

00:26:44.097 --> 00:26:47.328
I will be dead
in a matter of days.

00:26:55.542 --> 00:26:57.840
It's your turn!

00:26:57.877 --> 00:26:59.970
There's no open shot.

00:27:00.013 --> 00:27:01.878
Why don't you call a safety,
Neelix?

00:27:01.915 --> 00:27:03.940
Isn't that what you always do?

00:27:05.051 --> 00:27:06.518
I'm no coward.

00:27:06.553 --> 00:27:07.747
That's not what I've heard.

00:27:07.787 --> 00:27:09.812
Wait! What are you doing?
It's my turn.

00:27:09.856 --> 00:27:11.153
You lost your chance to play.

00:27:11.191 --> 00:27:13.091
Now you're going to lose.

00:27:13.126 --> 00:27:15.594
Neelix, where did you go?

00:27:15.629 --> 00:27:17.426
Why did you leave us?

00:27:17.464 --> 00:27:19.728
I did what I thought was right.

00:27:19.766 --> 00:27:21.028
You were afraid!

00:27:21.067 --> 00:27:22.034
No!

00:27:22.068 --> 00:27:23.262
I!

00:27:23.303 --> 00:27:24.429
Neelix...

00:27:24.471 --> 00:27:25.563
Who are you?

00:27:25.605 --> 00:27:26.867
It's me, Palaxia.

00:27:26.906 --> 00:27:28.464
Palaxia?!

00:27:28.508 --> 00:27:31.341
Why weren't you here to help us?

00:27:37.217 --> 00:27:38.184
You!

00:27:38.218 --> 00:27:41.085
You did this, butcher!

00:27:45.592 --> 00:27:47.389
Bridge to Neelix.

00:27:52.332 --> 00:27:54.061
H-here, Captain.

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:56.261
Neelix,
I thought you'd like to know.

00:27:56.302 --> 00:27:58.736
We're approaching Rinax.

00:28:02.175 --> 00:28:03.540
Neelix?

00:28:05.679 --> 00:28:07.203
Thank you, Captain.

00:28:07.247 --> 00:28:09.909
I'll be right there.

00:28:09.983 --> 00:28:12.747
Entering synchronous orbit,
Captain.

00:28:24.664 --> 00:28:26.154
Hard to believe

00:28:26.199 --> 00:28:29.862
that on clear nights,
you could look up from Talax

00:28:29.903 --> 00:28:32.497
and see the shimmering lights
of the colony.

00:28:35.275 --> 00:28:37.072
The night of the cascade,

00:28:37.110 --> 00:28:41.069
a bright flash
cut across the sky.

00:28:41.114 --> 00:28:47.713
It was so blinding that people
threw themselves to the ground,

00:28:47.754 --> 00:28:53.590
and everything stopped
like a moment out of time.

00:28:53.626 --> 00:28:55.253
Then we all looked up

00:28:55.295 --> 00:28:59.527
to see where the flash
had come from,

00:28:59.566 --> 00:29:03.468
but the sky seemed oddly empty.

00:29:06.039 --> 00:29:07.836
It took most of us
a few seconds to realize

00:29:07.874 --> 00:29:12.208
it was because Rinax was...

00:29:12.245 --> 00:29:13.906
gone.

00:29:13.947 --> 00:29:15.915
Of course, the moon
was still there.

00:29:16.015 --> 00:29:19.678
We just couldn't see it
because of that metreon cloud.

00:29:19.719 --> 00:29:21.414
Engineering to Bridge.

00:29:21.454 --> 00:29:24.184
We're ready to begin transporter
presequencing, Captain.

00:29:24.224 --> 00:29:25.748
Acknowledged.

00:29:25.792 --> 00:29:29.250
This brings back
too many memories, Captain.

00:29:29.295 --> 00:29:31.286
If you'll excuse me?

00:29:39.773 --> 00:29:41.764
Of course.

00:29:46.946 --> 00:29:49.574
I believe I asked for
a larger container.

00:29:49.616 --> 00:29:51.311
Correct me if I'm wrong, Doctor,

00:29:51.351 --> 00:29:53.410
but we're talking
about a cloud sample

00:29:53.453 --> 00:29:56.217
large enough to contain
a few subatomic particles.

00:29:56.256 --> 00:29:57.223
Right?

00:29:57.257 --> 00:29:58.281
Yes, but the isotope

00:29:58.324 --> 00:30:00.292
accounts for just
a minuscule fraction

00:30:00.326 --> 00:30:01.691
of the cloud's total mass.

00:30:01.728 --> 00:30:03.286
I want to be sure we get enough.

00:30:03.329 --> 00:30:04.296
Don't worry.

00:30:04.330 --> 00:30:06.264
We do this all the time.

00:30:06.299 --> 00:30:07.960
Presequencing complete, Captain.

00:30:08.001 --> 00:30:09.969
I'm ready to begin transport.

00:30:10.069 --> 00:30:11.331
Proceed.

00:30:11.371 --> 00:30:13.134
Targeting scanners.

00:30:13.173 --> 00:30:15.903
Locking on.

00:30:15.942 --> 00:30:17.910
Energize.

00:30:22.949 --> 00:30:25.884
Captain, we have
the sample aboard.

00:30:25.919 --> 00:30:28.217
The containment field
is holding.

00:30:28.254 --> 00:30:30.279
Well done.
Janeway out.

00:30:30.323 --> 00:30:32.257
Good luck, Doctor.

00:30:46.339 --> 00:30:48.000
Neelix?

00:30:50.143 --> 00:30:52.441
Neelix, are you in here?

00:30:56.049 --> 00:30:57.448
Neelix?

00:31:03.590 --> 00:31:06.058
I've been looking
for you everywhere.

00:31:06.092 --> 00:31:08.959
Why did you take
your combadge off?

00:31:08.995 --> 00:31:11.486
I wanted to be alone.

00:31:11.531 --> 00:31:17.959
I'm sorry to bother you,
but I was worried.

00:31:18.004 --> 00:31:20.199
I know how you must feel.

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:23.175
No, you don't know.

00:31:23.209 --> 00:31:26.804
Not everything.

00:31:26.846 --> 00:31:32.284
You don't know where I was
the night Rinax was destroyed.

00:31:32.318 --> 00:31:37.847
On Talax, fighting with
the defense forces.

00:31:37.891 --> 00:31:39.415
I was on Talax,

00:31:39.459 --> 00:31:43.486
but I wasn't fighting
with the defense forces.

00:31:45.798 --> 00:31:48.323
I was hiding from them.

00:31:53.339 --> 00:31:57.105
I wasn't a hero at the Battle
of the Pyrithian Gorge.

00:31:59.612 --> 00:32:01.944
I've never even been there.

00:32:02.015 --> 00:32:05.348
I don't understand.

00:32:07.120 --> 00:32:09.748
I never reported for duty.

00:32:09.789 --> 00:32:11.347
Why not?

00:32:12.926 --> 00:32:15.019
I thought the war was unjust...

00:32:17.063 --> 00:32:19.122
that Talax was fighting
for reasons

00:32:19.165 --> 00:32:22.157
that weren't
worth killing for...

00:32:24.203 --> 00:32:27.297
or at least that's
what I told myself.

00:32:29.342 --> 00:32:31.276
But...

00:32:31.311 --> 00:32:33.404
the real reason
I didn't report...

00:32:38.351 --> 00:32:40.410
was because I was a coward.

00:32:42.922 --> 00:32:44.446
Now you know.

00:32:50.663 --> 00:32:53.496
If the Talaxian authorities
would have caught you,

00:32:53.533 --> 00:32:55.000
what would they have done?

00:32:55.101 --> 00:32:57.228
During wartime, punishment

00:32:57.270 --> 00:33:00.171
for refusing military service
was death.

00:33:00.206 --> 00:33:02.174
So...

00:33:02.208 --> 00:33:07.236
you put your life at risk
for something you believed in

00:33:07.280 --> 00:33:10.113
and you think
that makes you a coward.

00:33:10.149 --> 00:33:11.377
I don't understand.

00:33:11.417 --> 00:33:13.248
It makes me a liar.

00:33:13.286 --> 00:33:15.618
I'd lied about it
all these years...

00:33:15.655 --> 00:33:17.816
to you, to Jetrel, to everyone.

00:33:17.857 --> 00:33:19.848
Because you're dishonest.

00:33:21.394 --> 00:33:23.954
Because I'm ashamed.

00:33:23.997 --> 00:33:27.728
What an awful burden you've
carried all these years.

00:33:27.767 --> 00:33:30.395
No wonder you're
so angry with Jetrel.

00:33:30.436 --> 00:33:31.903
Of course I am.

00:33:31.938 --> 00:33:33.633
He killed them all...

00:33:33.673 --> 00:33:37.475
my mother, my father,
my little brothers.

00:33:37.608 --> 00:33:39.906
Is that really why?

00:33:41.512 --> 00:33:44.970
Ever since Jetrel came on board,
you've despised him.

00:33:45.015 --> 00:33:48.280
The hurt and anger
you've held in all these years

00:33:48.319 --> 00:33:49.911
was vented right at him,

00:33:49.954 --> 00:33:53.788
but was it really Jetrel
you're angry with?

00:33:53.824 --> 00:33:56.691
Is he the one you blame
for what happened?

00:33:56.727 --> 00:33:58.490
I don't know.

00:33:58.529 --> 00:34:02.192
Or was he just
a convenient target

00:34:02.233 --> 00:34:05.691
to keep you from looking
somewhere else?

00:34:05.736 --> 00:34:07.931
You mean...

00:34:09.373 --> 00:34:12.342
from looking at myself?

00:34:16.814 --> 00:34:21.217
You may be right...

00:34:21.252 --> 00:34:23.516
but I hate him

00:34:23.554 --> 00:34:27.183
and I don't think
I can stop hating him.

00:34:27.224 --> 00:34:31.126
Maybe you have to stop
hating yourself first.

00:34:47.111 --> 00:34:49.841
Are we ready to begin synthesis?

00:34:49.880 --> 00:34:55.250
Computer, override Command
1-EMH-Alpha and end program.

00:34:55.286 --> 00:34:56.275
Now just wait one...

00:35:30.788 --> 00:35:31.755
Dr. Jetrel.

00:35:31.789 --> 00:35:33.188
You startled me.

00:35:33.223 --> 00:35:35.282
I'm sorry, but I need
to speak with you...

00:35:35.326 --> 00:35:38.090
Is it possible,
we-we could talk later?

00:35:38.128 --> 00:35:41.120
It won't be long
before I am too weak to work.

00:35:41.165 --> 00:35:43.656
I would like to finish
before I die.

00:35:48.405 --> 00:35:49.394
What is that?

00:35:51.642 --> 00:35:55.840
You're engaging in some kind of
bizarre experiment, aren't you?

00:35:55.879 --> 00:35:57.278
What is it this time?

00:35:57.314 --> 00:35:58.576
You don't understand.

00:35:58.616 --> 00:35:59.913
I can help them.

00:36:01.785 --> 00:36:03.912
What do you call that?

00:36:03.954 --> 00:36:05.922
Scientific progress?

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:08.788
I'm going to the Captain.

00:36:16.767 --> 00:36:19.235
Any report on Dr. Jetrel's
progress, Commander?

00:36:19.269 --> 00:36:20.395
Not yet, Captain.

00:36:20.437 --> 00:36:21.563
Bridge to Sick Bay.

00:36:21.605 --> 00:36:22.799
Janeway to Sick Bay.

00:36:25.009 --> 00:36:28.035
Janeway to Dr. Jetrel.

00:36:29.413 --> 00:36:31.506
Computer, activate Emergency
Medical Hologram.

00:36:31.548 --> 00:36:32.776
Please, state the nature...

00:36:32.816 --> 00:36:34.875
What the hell is going on
down there, Doctor?

00:36:34.918 --> 00:36:37.409
Dr. Jetrel deactivated me.

00:36:37.454 --> 00:36:38.580
He's gone now.

00:36:38.622 --> 00:36:40.920
Computer, locate Dr. Jetrel.

00:36:40.958 --> 00:36:43.950
Dr. Jetrel
is in Transporter Room 1.

00:36:44.061 --> 00:36:46.461
And Mr. Neelix is unconscious.

00:36:46.497 --> 00:36:48.863
It appears he's been
tranquilized.

00:36:50.567 --> 00:36:51.898
You have the Bridge, Commander.

00:36:51.935 --> 00:36:53.493
Security, meet me
in Transporter Room 1.

00:36:53.537 --> 00:36:54.526
Tuvok.

00:37:02.680 --> 00:37:04.910
Please, step aside, Doctor.

00:37:04.982 --> 00:37:06.074
You must let me continue.

00:37:06.116 --> 00:37:07.515
Lives depend on it.

00:37:07.551 --> 00:37:09.576
We've heard that
from you before.

00:37:09.620 --> 00:37:11.315
You're beginning
to lose credibility.

00:37:11.355 --> 00:37:13.482
Captain, I beg you.

00:37:13.524 --> 00:37:15.116
Let me bring them back.

00:37:15.159 --> 00:37:16.319
Bring who back?

00:37:16.360 --> 00:37:19.090
Who, Doctor?

00:37:19.129 --> 00:37:21.791
Who is it you're going
to bring back?

00:37:21.832 --> 00:37:25.233
The victims of Rinax.

00:37:34.211 --> 00:37:37.146
He's out of his mind, Captain.

00:37:37.181 --> 00:37:40.810
Please, look at my calculations.

00:37:40.851 --> 00:37:44.082
You remember what I told you
about metremia, Captain?

00:37:44.121 --> 00:37:47.090
How it causes the body's
atomic structure

00:37:47.124 --> 00:37:48.455
to undergo fission.

00:37:49.993 --> 00:37:52.359
It mirrors the way

00:37:52.396 --> 00:37:55.695
the metreon cascade
vaporized its victims...

00:37:55.733 --> 00:37:58.167
through biomolecular
disintegration.

00:37:58.202 --> 00:38:01.137
Do we have to listen to this?

00:38:01.171 --> 00:38:04.197
I think we should hear him out.

00:38:04.241 --> 00:38:07.301
What I've been working on
for the past 15 years

00:38:07.344 --> 00:38:10.905
is a way to rebuild
that atomic structure.

00:38:10.981 --> 00:38:13.677
What I call regenerative fusion.

00:38:13.717 --> 00:38:14.706
Are you saying

00:38:14.752 --> 00:38:19.246
you're actually trying
to... restore

00:38:19.289 --> 00:38:22.452
people who were vaporized
by the metreon cascade?

00:38:22.493 --> 00:38:23.585
Yes.

00:38:25.529 --> 00:38:27.156
Given the degree
of fragmentation

00:38:27.197 --> 00:38:28.255
you're talking about,

00:38:28.298 --> 00:38:29.925
I don't see
how that's possible.

00:38:29.967 --> 00:38:32.629
The electrostatic properties
of the cloud are such

00:38:32.669 --> 00:38:34.432
that the disassembled
bio-matter

00:38:34.471 --> 00:38:37.929
has been held in a state
of animated suspension.

00:38:38.041 --> 00:38:41.875
I discovered years ago
that reintegration is possible.

00:38:41.912 --> 00:38:45.279
Is that what you were doing
with that thing in Sick Bay?

00:38:45.315 --> 00:38:46.680
Yes, exactly.

00:38:46.717 --> 00:38:47.945
Neelix saw it.

00:38:48.051 --> 00:38:49.848
It was an amalgamation

00:38:49.887 --> 00:38:51.855
of randomly fused
organic material.

00:38:51.889 --> 00:38:55.586
Bits and pieces of previously
vaporized bio-matter.

00:38:55.626 --> 00:38:59.960
But if the bio-matter
in the cloud is so random,

00:39:00.063 --> 00:39:02.031
so jumbled,

00:39:02.065 --> 00:39:05.330
how could you reconstruct
something whole?

00:39:05.369 --> 00:39:08.202
I used medical records
to identify the genetic coding

00:39:08.238 --> 00:39:11.537
of a specific victim...
a test case, if you will.

00:39:11.575 --> 00:39:15.011
Once we input his DNA sequence,

00:39:15.045 --> 00:39:19.209
then... we can isolate
his atomic fragments

00:39:19.249 --> 00:39:24.482
with your targeting scanners,
and then rematerialize him.

00:39:25.522 --> 00:39:27.149
What if he's right?

00:39:27.191 --> 00:39:29.159
Captain, Dr. Jetrel is proposing

00:39:29.193 --> 00:39:31.161
the reconstruction
and reanimation

00:39:31.195 --> 00:39:33.823
of a remarkably complex set
of bio-systems

00:39:33.864 --> 00:39:36.594
from billions
of subatomic particles.

00:39:38.302 --> 00:39:39.929
I'm afraid I have to agree.

00:39:40.037 --> 00:39:43.165
It all sounds very implausible.

00:39:46.076 --> 00:39:49.512
You sound exactly
like my country.

00:39:51.748 --> 00:39:55.411
I asked them for more funds
to continue my research

00:39:55.452 --> 00:39:58.785
to help the victims of Rinax

00:39:58.822 --> 00:40:04.692
because I wanted the world
to know I am not a monster.

00:40:04.728 --> 00:40:10.325
My theories can be used
to heal as well as to destroy.

00:40:10.367 --> 00:40:13.962
But they... they refused me.

00:40:14.071 --> 00:40:17.666
Called me a Talaxian sympathizer

00:40:17.708 --> 00:40:19.409
and exiled me.

00:40:19.409 --> 00:40:19.704
And exiled me.

00:40:22.112 --> 00:40:25.138
Does Neelix
really have metremia?

00:40:25.182 --> 00:40:29.516
Or was that just a pretext
for getting us to come to Rinax?

00:40:31.088 --> 00:40:33.818
It was just a pretext, Captain.

00:40:33.857 --> 00:40:36.792
You do not have metremia,
Neelix.

00:40:36.827 --> 00:40:38.692
You are not going to die.

00:40:44.034 --> 00:40:47.136
Why didn't you just tell us
the truth in the first place?

00:40:47.269 --> 00:40:49.430
Your Captain is
an accomplished scientist.

00:40:49.471 --> 00:40:51.439
She doubts my theories.

00:40:51.473 --> 00:40:53.737
My own government did, too.

00:40:53.775 --> 00:40:57.575
I just could not risk
being not believed again.

00:40:57.613 --> 00:40:59.774
But...

00:40:59.815 --> 00:41:04.377
Captain, it will work.

00:41:04.419 --> 00:41:09.186
Captain, if there's
any chance he can do it,

00:41:09.224 --> 00:41:13.126
you've got to let him try.

00:41:13.162 --> 00:41:17.098
Neelix, there are just
too many variables.

00:41:17.132 --> 00:41:19.396
Please, Captain.

00:41:25.941 --> 00:41:29.900
Lieutenant Tuvok, activate
the emergency containment field

00:41:29.945 --> 00:41:31.913
around the transporter pad.

00:41:31.947 --> 00:41:33.471
Aye, Captain.

00:41:36.485 --> 00:41:38.476
Emergency containment
field activated.

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:40.488
We'll have to retarget scanners

00:41:40.522 --> 00:41:42.422
to the widest possible
confinement beam.

00:41:42.457 --> 00:41:45.085
It's our only hope
of achieving bond cohesion

00:41:45.127 --> 00:41:47.095
with such broadly
scattered fragments.

00:41:47.129 --> 00:41:48.994
Retargeting scanners
to wide beam.

00:41:49.097 --> 00:41:50.155
Energize.

00:41:52.334 --> 00:41:54.598
Phase transition coils
to maximum.

00:41:54.636 --> 00:41:55.898
Aye, Captain.

00:41:59.041 --> 00:42:01.475
Is the bio-genic field
operational?

00:42:01.510 --> 00:42:02.909
Affirmative.

00:42:02.945 --> 00:42:04.537
It's incredible.

00:42:05.914 --> 00:42:08.883
Atomic cohesion has dropped
to 49 percent, Captain.

00:42:08.917 --> 00:42:10.544
Pattern buffers
to maximum power.

00:42:10.586 --> 00:42:12.053
They are already at 100 percent.

00:42:12.087 --> 00:42:13.384
Take them to 120, Lieutenant.

00:42:13.422 --> 00:42:16.391
Pattern buffers to 120 percent
of rated maximum.

00:42:16.425 --> 00:42:17.722
We're losing him.

00:42:17.759 --> 00:42:22.219
Atomic cohesion to 39 percent...
22 percent... 14 percent.

00:42:22.264 --> 00:42:24.232
His pattern is
degrading rapidly.

00:42:24.266 --> 00:42:26.860
You must increase the power
to the pattern buffers, Captain!

00:42:26.902 --> 00:42:28.267
We've got to stimulate cohesion.

00:42:28.303 --> 00:42:30.362
Is there any way to augment
the bio-genic field?

00:42:30.405 --> 00:42:32.498
The degree of fragmentation
is simply too great.

00:42:32.541 --> 00:42:34.441
It will not work.

00:42:34.476 --> 00:42:37.036
We are overloading the system.

00:42:42.551 --> 00:42:45.349
Shut it down, Mr. Tuvok.

00:43:01.336 --> 00:43:05.773
Captain's Log, Stardate 48840.5.

00:43:05.807 --> 00:43:09.436
Dr. Jetrel's metremia is
now in its final stage.

00:43:09.478 --> 00:43:12.811
He's spending his
remaining hours in Sick Bay.

00:43:29.298 --> 00:43:31.789
Neelix...

00:43:33.302 --> 00:43:39.969
I suppose you think this is
a fitting punishment for me.

00:43:42.511 --> 00:43:47.278
Maybe the cascade was
a punishment for all of us,

00:43:47.316 --> 00:43:50.683
for our hatred,

00:43:50.719 --> 00:43:53.552
our brutality.

00:43:54.556 --> 00:43:57.616
There's something
I need to tell you.

00:43:59.394 --> 00:44:01.919
I tried to tell you before,
but...

00:44:04.333 --> 00:44:06.699
Wha... what is it?

00:44:06.735 --> 00:44:09.169
I want to tell you...

00:44:11.540 --> 00:44:13.838
that I forgive you.
